[THOUGHT] Mid-luteal progesterone is normal — confirming ovulation occurred recently. This rules out anovulation as the cause of infertility. With normal ovarian reserve markers (FSH, estradiol), normal thyroid and prolactin-adjacent hormones (TSH normal; prolactin unavailable but no clinical signs reported), normal pelvic anatomy, and confirmed ovulation, the remaining major categories to evaluate are:  
- Tubal patency (e.g., hysterosalpingography — HSG), since blocked tubes are a common cause of primary infertility;  
- Cervical factors (e.g., postcoital test — now rarely used);  
- Unexplained infertility (diagnosis of exclusion);  
- But before HSG, I must assess for risk factors or symptoms suggesting prior pelvic infection (e.g., PID, chlamydia) — which is the leading cause of tubal damage. Also, I have not yet gathered the patient’s past medical/surgical history, medications, allergies, or social/exposure factors — all essential domains per the Evidence Framework. I’ve covered domains 1 (chief complaint), 2 (course — partially, but need more), 3 (associated symptoms — not yet assessed), and 8 (some labs/imaging), but domains 4 (past medical/surgical history), 5 (meds/allergies), and 6 (social/occupational/exposures) remain completely unexplored. These are mandatory and may reveal critical clues (e.g., prior STIs, appendectomy, endometriosis surgery, smoking, chemotherapy exposure).

[QUERY] Have you ever been diagnosed with or treated for any chronic medical conditions — such as diabetes, autoimmune diseases, thyroid disorders, or pelvic inflammatory disease? And have you had any surgeries, especially involving the abdomen or pelvis?

